          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white">About the Chinese Gender Predictor</h2>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The Chinese Gender Predictor is a traditional tool used for centuries in Chinese culture to estimate the likelihood of conceiving a baby boy or girl. Rooted in ancient Chinese philosophy, it draws from the principles of Yin and Yang, the five elements, and the lunar calendar to make its predictions.
          </p>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Historically, the chart is believed to have been discovered in a royal tomb near Beijing, dating back over 700 years. For generations, families across China and East Asia have referenced this chart as part of family planning traditions, passing it down through families as a cultural keepsake rather than a medical instrument.
          </p>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Today, the Chinese Gender Predictor continues to attract interest from expecting parents worldwide who enjoy exploring the ancient method alongside modern medical knowledge. Whether viewed as a fun tradition or a cultural curiosity, it remains one of the most recognized gender prediction tools in history. Learn more about <a href="#how-it-works" class="text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 underline underline-offset-2">how the chart works</a> below.
          </p>
        </article>

        <!-- How the Chinese Gender Chart Works -->
        <article id="how-it-works" class="space-y-5">
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white">How the Chinese Gender Chart Works</h2>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The Chinese Gender Chart operates on two primary inputs: the mother's age at the time of conception and the lunar month in which conception takes place. Both values are mapped onto a grid to reveal whether the chart predicts a boy or a girl.
          </p>
          <div class="space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Mother's Age</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              In the traditional system, the mother's age refers to her Chinese lunar age (虚岁), which is calculated differently from the Western age. The Chinese lunar age is typically one year higher than the Western age because it counts time spent in the womb as the first year of life.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Target Year and Lunar Calendar</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              The Chinese lunar calendar differs from the Gregorian calendar used in most of the world. Each lunar month begins on a new moon and lasts approximately 29.5 days. The chart considers which lunar month the conception falls within, not the corresponding Gregorian month.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The Prediction Process</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              To use the chart, find the mother's lunar age on one axis and the target lunar month on the other. The intersection point indicates the predicted gender. Our calculator automates this process by converting Gregorian dates to lunar dates and cross-referencing the chart accurately.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- Is the Chinese Gender Predictor Accurate? -->
        <article class="space-y-5">
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white">Is the Chinese Gender Predictor Accurate?</h2>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The accuracy of the Chinese Gender Predictor is a subject of ongoing debate. Anecdotal reports suggest the chart is correct roughly 50% of the time — which is essentially the same probability as any other random guess. There is no scientific evidence to support its reliability as a predictor of baby gender.
          </p>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Many families report satisfying results when using the chart, but this is often attributed to confirmation bias — remembering correct predictions while forgetting incorrect ones. With a binary outcome (boy or girl), even random chance produces correct results half the time.
          </p>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Despite the lack of scientific backing, the chart remains popular as a cultural tradition and entertainment tool. Many parents enjoy comparing the chart's prediction against their actual results, treating it as a fun part of the pregnancy journey rather than a serious medical tool.
          </p>
        </article>

        <!-- Scientific Perspective -->
        <article class="space-y-5">
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white">Scientific Perspective</h2>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            From a scientific standpoint, there is no evidence that the Chinese Gender Predictor can reliably determine baby gender. Biological sex is determined at the moment of conception by the sperm cell carrying either an X chromosome (resulting in a girl) or a Y chromosome (resulting in a boy). No calendar-based method can influence or predict this genetic event.
          </p>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The probability of conceiving a boy or girl is approximately 50/50, and factors such as the mother's age, lunar month, diet, or timing do not significantly alter this ratio. Scientific studies have consistently shown that external variables have minimal to no impact on which sperm fertilizes the egg.
          </p>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            This calculator is designed purely for entertainment and educational purposes. It should not be used to make medical decisions or to determine actual pregnancy outcomes. Always consult a healthcare provider for accurate information about fetal development and gender determination. Have more questions? Check our <a href="#faq" class="text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 underline underline-offset-2">FAQ section</a> below.
