            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-indigo-400 mt-1 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
              <div>
                <strong class="text-white">Ultrasound (18–22 weeks):</strong> A mid-pregnancy anatomy scan is the most common way to visually determine gender. Trained sonographers can identify physical differences with high accuracy.
              </div>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-purple-400 mt-1 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
              <div>
                <strong class="text-white">Cell-Free DNA Test (10+ weeks):</strong> A blood test that analyzes fetal DNA in the mother's bloodstream can detect the presence of a Y chromosome as early as 10 weeks into pregnancy.
              </div>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-pink-400 mt-1 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
              <div>
                <strong class="text-white">CVS or Amniocentesis:</strong> These diagnostic tests, typically performed for medical reasons, can also determine chromosomal sex with near certainty.
              </div>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Always discuss your options with your healthcare provider to choose the approach that is safe and appropriate for your situation.
          </p>
        </article>
